<Layout pageTitle="RPNI Design: Model, Evidence &amp; Verification" description="Equations, assumptions, parameter provenance and primary evidence for the regenerative peripheral nerve interface design laboratory." wide={true} compactHeader={true}>
<article class="rp-lab rp-methods">
 <header><span class="rp-eyebrow">RPNI Design &amp; Testing Laboratory / model {MODEL_VERSION}</span><h1>Model, evidence &amp; numerical verification</h1><p class="rp-intro">A transparent design experiment for the muscle-graft regenerative peripheral nerve interface: specified anatomy, prescribed muscle sources, calculated extracellular pickup, and a separate transport benchmark.</p></header>
 <nav aria-label="Methods sections"><a href="/simulations/rpni-design/">← Open the laboratory</a><a href="#scope">Scope</a><a href="#electrical">Electrical equations</a><a href="#regeneration">Biological clocks</a><a href="#transport">Transport</a><a href="#parameters">Parameters</a><a href="#verification">Verification</a><a href="#sources">Primary studies</a></nav>
 <div class="rp-model-note"><span class="rp-badge">Exploratory / uncalibrated</span><p>The laboratory implements an original reduced forward model. It has not been fitted to an RPNI recording dataset or validated for clinical outcomes, implant safety, graft viability, muscle force, sensory perception or prosthesis control.</p></div>
 <section id="scope"><h2>1. What the laboratory represents</h2><p>A muscle-graft regenerative peripheral nerve interface supplies a terminal nerve or separated fascicle with a free muscle target. After reinnervation, muscle-derived electrical activity can be recorded by electrodes. This laboratory represents that nerve-to-muscle recording architecture with idealized graft modules and synthetic equivalent motor units. <a href="#source-kung-2014">[{sourceNumber('kung-2014')}]</a> <a href="#source-vu-2020">[{sourceNumber('vu-2020')}]</a></p><p>{MODEL_SCOPE.purpose} All outputs are conditional on the selected geometry, source activity and tissue assumptions. No patient-specific fit, graft-survival prediction, clinical efficacy estimate, stimulation safety calculation or prosthetic task-accuracy prediction is supplied.</p><p>The signal is muscle-derived EMG. The model does not simply multiply an extracellular nerve signal by a gain. It prescribes muscle sources after assumed arrival, computes their extracellular potentials and samples those potentials with finite contacts. A nerve bridge, regenerative sieve, TEENI construct, targeted muscle reinnervation, dermal interface or composite RPNI requires different anatomy and equations.</p><div class="rp-table-scroll"><table><thead><tr><th>Published observation</th><th>Use in this laboratory</th><th>Limit on interpretation</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>30 × 15 × 5 mm harvested grafts in one human feasibility study.</td><td>A labeled starting geometry for an idealized placement envelope.</td><td>Harvest dimensions are not mature graft measurements or an optimized implant prescription.</td></tr><tr><td>Rat graft-mass groups differed in regenerated tissue and function.</td><td>Motivation to inspect transport distance alongside electrical pickup.</td><td>No conversion to a human thickness cutoff or a survival probability.</td></tr><tr><td>Human implanted prosthesis-control studies.</td><td>Motivation for independent source pools and multichannel recording.</td><td>RPNIs were part of systems containing residual-muscle channels, hardware and decoders. Those results do not calibrate geometry-to-task accuracy.</td></tr></tbody></table></div><p>Sources: <a href="#source-vu-2020">Vu 2020</a>, <a href="#source-hu-2021">Hu 2021</a>, <a href="#source-vu-2023">Vu 2023</a>, <a href="#source-wallace-2026">Wallace 2026</a>.</p></section>
 <section><h2>2. Geometry, routing and equivalent motor units</h2><p>The design contains one to four rectangular muscle-graft placement envelopes. All share the selected length, width and thickness. The x axis follows muscle fibers, y separates modules, and z spans thickness; every coordinate is in millimeters. Spacing is the edge-to-edge gap. Each graft receives a terminal nerve and one bipolar recording pair.</p><p>Seeded equivalent motor units occupy each graft. Three equally weighted, parallel representative fibers sample each unit’s spatial territory. This is a reduced source quadrature, not a reconstruction of every muscle fiber or a measured motor-unit census. Increasing the number of equivalent units changes total source power. Holding unit count fixed while enlarging a graft tests a different hypothesis from holding source density fixed.</p><p>Physical graft identity and source command identity are distinct. The same-pool allocation parameter is the probability that a source in graft i receives command label i; remaining assignments go to other labels. This is a synthetic routing stress test, not a surgical sorting-success probability. With one graft, only one command label exists.</p><p>The nerve-tip position determines the assumed straight-line regrowth distance. Nerve diameter is a geometric envelope; it does not determine motor-axon count, fascicular anatomy or electrical amplitude. The model does not solve Schwann-cell guidance, intramuscular branching mechanics, target recognition or failure to establish a functional synapse.</p><p>Geometry normalization keeps finite contact disks within the graft planform and bounds nerve dimensions, insertion depth, territory radius and pole spacing. A size change can therefore alter dependent parameters. Every sweep retains its effective configuration. Zero or overlapping contact spacing is available as a mathematical control and is labeled when it cannot represent two separate physical electrodes.</p></section>
 <section id="electrical"><h2>3. Electrical forward calculation</h2><h3>3.1 Quasi-static anisotropic volume conduction</h3><p>The conductor is infinite and has constant conductivity tensor Σ = diag(σ<sub>L</sub>, σ<sub>T</sub>, σ<sub>T</sub>). An impressed point current produces the Green-function potential below, with a zero-potential limit at infinity:</p><div class="rp-equation">φ(r) = I / [4π √det(Σ) √((r − r<sub>s</sub>)ᵀ Σ⁻¹ (r − r<sub>s</sub>))]</div><p>With current in amperes, distance in meters and conductivity in S/m, the result is volts. If all conductivities equal σ, the expression reduces to I/(4πσr). Transforming coordinates by Σ⁻¹ᐟ² gives the isotropic Poisson problem and its Jacobian factor √det(Σ). Published EMG forward models provide precedents for separating source, conductor and detector, including more detailed layered and nonuniform domains. <a href="#source-farina-2001">[{sourceNumber('farina-2001')}]</a> <a href="#source-mesin-2006">[{sourceNumber('mesin-2006')}]</a></p>
 <p>The implemented source has an explicit smooth core. Let σ<sub>g</sub> = (σ<sub>L</sub>σ<sub>T</sub>²)¹ᐟ³. With coordinates and core radius a<sub>s</sub> in mm, source current I<sub>µA</sub> in µA and potential in µV:</p><div class="rp-equation">φ<sub>µV</sub> = 1000 I<sub>µA</sub> / [4π √(σ<sub>L</sub>σ<sub>T</sub>²) √(Δx²/σ<sub>L</sub> + (Δy² + Δz²)/σ<sub>T</sub> + a<sub>s</sub>²/σ<sub>g</sub>)]</div><p>The factor 1000 combines the mm, µA and µV conversions. A zero core is the exact point-source solution away from its singularity. A positive core is a near-field approximation in conductivity-transformed coordinates; it is not a membrane radius. Near-contact results depend on this regularization.</p><p><strong>The graft outline is not an electrical boundary.</strong> It bounds source placement while the same conductor fills all space. No fat layer, host-muscle discontinuity, scar shell, insulating wrap, saline pocket or skin surface is resolved. Current scaling is linear; uniformly multiplying all conductivities by b divides voltage by b.</p>
 <h3>3.2 Current-balanced traveling muscle sources</h3><p>An event launches two traveling tripoles in opposite x directions from each representative fiber’s endplate. Each tripole has weights (+1, −2, +1), so instantaneous impressed current sums to zero. The two directions each receive half the unit current scale, distributed over three weighted fibers, with a seeded source-strength multiplier.</p><div class="rp-equation">I<sub>p</sub>(t) = [½ I<sub>0</sub> s<sub>i</sub> w<sub>f</sub> B(t) E(t)] × (1, −2, 1); &nbsp; Σ I<sub>p</sub>(t) = 0</div><p>B and E are smooth onset and endpoint tapers; a common taper multiplies every pole in a tripole, preserving balance as a wave enters or leaves its finite fiber. Muscle wave speed is in m/s, numerically equal to mm/ms. It is distinct from axonal regrowth in mm/day. The slow source-maturation factor multiplies the completed waveform.</p><p>These are prescribed equivalent sources. The laboratory does not solve muscle membrane channels, the action-potential cable equation, tendon current redistribution, motor-unit force or fatigue. Current-balanced spatial sources should not be confused with charge-balanced stimulation pulses. No stimulation threshold is calculated.</p>
 <h3>3.3 Finite contact integration and bipolar recording</h3><div class="rp-equation">φ̄<sub>A</sub>(t) = (1/|A|) ∫<sub>A</sub> φ(r,t) dA; &nbsp; v<sub>AB</sub>(t) = φ̄<sub>A</sub>(t) − φ̄<sub>B</sub>(t)</div><p>A contact is an ideal disk in the x–y plane. Gauss–Legendre integration in squared radius and equally spaced angular points average potential over its area. Order n uses 4n² samples. A zero-radius contact is a point detector. Identical coincident contacts cancel exactly; swapping their order reverses polarity without changing RMS.</p><p>This is an ideal high-impedance sensing approximation. Electrode polarization, material electrochemistry, contact impedance, amplifier loading, frequency filtering, motion artifact and lead mechanics are absent. A primary electrode comparison motivates contact design as a separate variable but used native rat muscle; its ranking is not adopted as a universal regenerated-graft result. <a href="#source-sando-2016">[{sourceNumber('sando-2016')}]</a></p></section>
 <section id="regeneration"><h2>4. Arrival, maturation and two time scales</h2><p>For territory i, d<sub>i</sub> is the straight-line distance from the terminal nerve tip. The chosen delay t<sub>0</sub>, regrowth speed v<sub>a</sub> and maturation constant τ<sub>m</sub> define:</p><div class="rp-equation">t<sub>a,i</sub> = t<sub>0</sub> + d<sub>i</sub>/v<sub>a</sub><br/>m<sub>i</sub>(t) = 0 for t ≤ t<sub>a,i</sub><br/>m<sub>i</sub>(t) = a [1 − exp(−(t − t<sub>a,i</sub>)/τ<sub>m</sub>)] for t &gt; t<sub>a,i</sub></div><p>Zero regrowth speed assigns no arrival. At the arrival instant the maturation gain is still zero. The mean source-maturation readout averages m over the original equivalent-unit cohort. It is an amplitude factor, not a proportion of histologically confirmed NMJs, viable cells or functional motor units.</p><p>The model does not represent early graft degeneration and muscle rebuilding in detail. Its monotonic gain is an explicit hypothesis. Availability a is independently imposed and does not come from the oxygen panel. Published culture-based constructs are distinguished from free muscle grafts in the evidence ledger. <a href="#source-urbanchek-2016">[{sourceNumber('urbanchek-2016')}]</a></p><p><strong>Biological replay uses days; electrical replay uses milliseconds.</strong> The anatomy can replay assumed regrowth over the biological horizon. The voltage traces remain tied to the chosen recording day. Playing or scrubbing electrical activity returns anatomy to that day, so its source activation corresponds to the displayed recording.</p></section>
 <section><h2>5. Signal statistics and source separation</h2><h3>Prescribed activation</h3><p>Equivalent units have recruitment thresholds spanning zero to 0.8. Above threshold, the selected drive determines a firing rate between the chosen minimum and maximum. Events follow independent seeded Poisson processes. Fibers within one unit share events. Refractoriness, common synaptic drive, spike synchrony, adaptation and fatigue are absent. Target and other command pools have separately adjustable drive.</p><h3>Expected source power</h3><p>For channel c and source i, h<sub>ci</sub> is the bipolar waveform, λ<sub>i</sub> is event rate in Hz and m<sub>i</sub> is slow source gain. Centered variance under independent stationary Poisson events is:</p><div class="rp-equation">P<sub>c</sub> = Σ<sub>i</sub> λ<sub>i</sub> m<sub>i</sub>² ∫ h<sub>ci</sub>(s)² ds<br/>C<sub>cd</sub> = Σ<sub>i</sub> λ<sub>i</sub> m<sub>i</sub>² ∫ h<sub>ci</sub>(s) h<sub>di</sub>(s) ds</div><p>The integral has units µV²·s; multiplying by event rate in s⁻¹ gives variance in µV². The source matrix takes the square root of power separately for each recording channel and command pool at the stated drive and biological age. It is not a dimensionless gain. The channel-similarity matrix is signed C<sub>cd</sub>/√(C<sub>cc</sub>C<sub>dd</sub>), undefined when either channel has no clean variance.</p><p>Expected clean RMS excludes acquisition noise. Expected total RMS adds the imposed noise variance. The target power fraction P<sub>T</sub>/(P<sub>T</sub> + P<sub>O</sub>) also excludes noise. Perfect routing can produce a selective channel; correlated biological commands, absent here, could still correlate real recordings.</p><h3>A finite synthetic recording</h3><p>Source waveforms use a finer internal grid set by the kernel-oversampling factor. Seeded event times are convolved with these waveforms using linear interpolation onto the selected recording grid. Expected power and covariance integrate the same piecewise-linear kernel exactly segment by segment. Kernel oversampling refines the source waveform and expected power; it does not provide an antialias filter for the recording output. Fast, narrow sources can require refinement of both grids. Each command-pool trace is demeaned over the window. Independent Gaussian channel noise is added and demeaned. For these traces, RMS = √(Σv²/N). Because finite target and other traces can covary, actual clean RMS uses the summed trace rather than replacing it with a quadrature sum.</p><p>Clean/noise SNR is 20 log₁₀(RMS<sub>clean</sub>/RMS<sub>noise</sub>). “Clean” includes target and other command pools: high SNR does not guarantee selectivity. Zero signal and zero noise receive explicit states rather than a fabricated finite decibel value. Rat studies used different signal definitions, including integrated-EMG active/rest ratios; their values are not interchangeable with this metric. <a href="#source-woo-2014">[{sourceNumber('woo-2014')}]</a> <a href="#source-frost-2018">[{sourceNumber('frost-2018')}]</a></p><p>Finite recordings fluctuate; window-mean removal also lowers expected squared RMS, especially for short windows. Sweeps and repeat-seed studies use expected source variance, while the trace panel reports the actual finite realization. Neither metric is a classifier accuracy, mutual information, clinical recovery measure or prosthesis score.</p></section>
 <section id="transport"><h2>6. Independent steady transport diagnostic</h2><p>A slab of full thickness h has the same fixed concentration c<sub>b</sub> at both faces. Diffusivity D is in mm²/s and first-order consumption k is in s⁻¹. No supply from side faces or internal vessels is included.</p><div class="rp-equation">D c″ − k c = 0; &nbsp; c(−h/2) = c(h/2) = c<sub>b</sub><br/>λ = √(D/k); &nbsp; c(z)/c<sub>b</sub> = cosh(z/λ)/cosh(h/(2λ))<br/>c(0)/c<sub>b</sub> = sech(h/(2λ)); &nbsp; c̄/c<sub>b</sub> = tanh(h/(2λ))/(h/(2λ))</div><p>At k = 0, both concentration ratios are one. The implementation uses an algebraically equivalent exponential form to avoid overflow at large h/λ. The normalized result does not give measured tissue oxygen tension or absolute concentration. Only h√(k/D)/2 determines the ratios, so a profile alone cannot identify D and k independently.</p><p>At the initial h = 5 mm, D = 0.002 mm²/s and k = 0.01 s⁻¹, the exact hypothetical center/face ratio is about 0.0075 and the thickness mean about 0.179. These are consequences of the selected avascular slab assumptions. A mature graft’s vascular network invalidates interpreting this as its day-120 oxygen measurement.</p><p><strong>There is no concentration threshold or oxygen-to-survival mapping.</strong> The diagnostic never multiplies electrical source availability or maturation. Thus the page can show a low slab ratio alongside mature modeled sources without asserting that such a tissue state is biologically viable. A coupled model would require measured vessel distribution, consumption kinetics, remodeling and independently validated oxygen-history effects. The rat mass comparison motivates the question but does not supply those functions. <a href="#source-hu-2021">[{sourceNumber('hu-2021')}]</a></p></section>
 <section id="parameters"><h2>7. Parameters, units and provenance</h2><p>Ranges are computational exploration bounds. They are not manufacturing tolerances, anatomical norms or approved implant settings. Defaults marked “evidence-context” provide scale context; the model has no species-specific fitted parameter set.</p><div class="rp-table-scroll"><table><thead><tr><th>Parameter</th><th>Default</th><th>Range</th><th>Class &amp; interpretation</th></tr></thead><tbody>{Object.entries(PARAMETER_META).map(([key,p])=><tr><td>{p.label}<br/><code>{key}</code></td><td>{DEFAULT_CONFIG[key]} {p.unit}</td><td>{p.min}–{p.max} {p.unit}</td><td><span class="rp-source-label">{p.sourceClass}</span>{p.note}</td></tr>)}</tbody></table></div>
 {PARAMETER_PROVENANCE.map(p=><section><h3>{p.label}</h3><p><span class="rp-source-label">{p.classification}</span>{p.evidence}</p><p>{p.implementation} {p.references.map(id=><a href={'#source-'+id}>[{sourceNumber(id)}] </a>)}</p></section>)}</section>
 <section id="verification"><h2>8. Numerical verification and reproducibility</h2><p>Verification checks the declared equations and their implementation. Biological validation asks whether predictions agree with independent observations in a specified experimental domain. The latter has not been performed.</p>
 {verification?<div class="rp-verification-record"><p><strong>{verification.tests.passed} of {verification.tests.count} named tests passed:</strong> 40 numerical checks and 8 experiment-format checks for model {MODEL_VERSION}. Source SHA-256 <code>{verification.engine.sha256}</code>. Recorded with {verification.environment.node} on {verification.environment.platform}. These results verify implementation, not biological predictions.</p><div class="rp-table-scroll"><table><thead><tr><th>Analytic or physical control</th><th>Measured error / residual</th><th>Tolerance</th><th>Result</th></tr></thead><tbody>{verification.checks.map(c=><tr><td>{c.label}</td><td>{Number(c.measured).toPrecision(4)} {c.unit}</td><td>{c.tolerance===0?'Exact zero':Number(c.tolerance).toExponential(1)}</td><td>{c.pass?'Passed':'Failed'}</td></tr>)}</tbody></table></div><h3>Retained resolution study</h3><div class="rp-table-scroll"><table><thead><tr><th>Resolution change</th><th>Channel</th><th>Expected clean RMS / µV</th><th>Relative change</th></tr></thead><tbody>{verification.convergence.map(c=><tr><td>{PARAMETER_META[c.parameter]?.label||c.parameter}: {c.coarse} → {c.fine}</td><td>{c.channel}</td><td>{Number(c.coarseValue).toFixed(6)} → {Number(c.fineValue).toFixed(6)}</td><td>{Number(c.relativeDifference*100).toPrecision(4)}%</td></tr>)}</tbody></table></div><p>For the retained paired-graft reference, refining the internal kernel from ×4 to ×8 changes expected clean RMS by less than 0.13% on either channel. This bound applies to that benchmark. A source close to a contact, smaller source core, faster wave or different geometry requires its own refinement.</p><p>The recorded default solve took {Number(verification.benchmarks.defaultRuntimeMs).toFixed(1)} ms in one local Node run with 48 equivalent units and two channels. Browser and hardware timings vary.</p><details><summary>Complete retained numerical verification record</summary><pre class="rp-verification-json">{JSON.stringify(verification,null,2)}</pre></details></div>:<p>The release includes a retained numerical verification record and runnable tests in the source repository.</p>}
 <div class="rp-check-grid"><div><h3>Physics limits</h3><p>Isotropic Green-function limit, units, current linearity, conductivity scaling and source superposition.</p></div><div><h3>Source and detector controls</h3><p>Instantaneous current balance, finite contact integration, coincident bipolar cancellation and polarity reversal.</p></div><div><h3>Resolution</h3><p>Contact quadrature and temporal refinement assess the declared discretization; extreme near-contact sources require their own refinement.</p></div><div><h3>Transport</h3><p>Exact face conditions, symmetry, zero-consumption limit, profile/mean agreement and the diffusion–reaction equation.</p></div><div><h3>Event and statistical limits</h3><p>Zero drive, zero source, zero regrowth, independent source power and finite-trace component accounting.</p></div><div><h3>Reproducible designs</h3><p>Seeded geometry and events, normalized geometry bounds, exact-version parameter restore and source-independent transport.</p></div></div>
 <h3>Controlled studies and uncertainty</h3><p>A sweep varies one requested parameter over five values with a common seed. Dependent geometry can normalize to different effective values, all retained. Seed studies vary the synthetic source realization at fixed parameters; their 10th–90th percentile interval excludes parameter uncertainty, patient variability, measurement error and model-form error. It is not a biological confidence interval.</p><p>Absolute amplitude is nonidentifiable from RMS alone: increasing current, decreasing conductivity, increasing source count or increasing source gain can compensate for each other. Before fitting, specify which parameters are independently measured and which endpoints will be tested. Fitting one waveform does not validate distant cross-talk, transport or human outcomes.</p>
 <h3>Exports and restore</h3><p>Experiment JSON retains the exact model version, normalized design, geometry, sources, waveform kernels, recordings, metrics, biological-age samples, transport solution, comparisons and assumptions. Recording CSV preserves each channel’s total, clean, target, other and noise samples in µV. Growth CSV preserves slow-time values and explicitly named expected metrics.</p><p>Restore accepts only the exact current format/model version and a complete finite numeric configuration satisfying supported bounds. Unknown keys, malformed values, incompatible versions and geometry-changing normalization are rejected. Saved result objects are ignored and recomputed. Display state, pinned references and previous studies are retained in the export for inspection but are not silently restored.</p><p><a href="https://github.com/corbins-cryo-pod/buildthesimulation/blob/main/src/lib/rpni-design/model.mjs" target="_blank" rel="noreferrer">Inspect the numerical engine</a> · <a href="https://github.com/corbins-cryo-pod/buildthesimulation/blob/main/tests/rpni-design.mjs" target="_blank" rel="noreferrer">Inspect the numerical tests</a> · <a href="https://github.com/corbins-cryo-pod/buildthesimulation/blob/main/docs/rpni-design/SCIENCE.md" target="_blank" rel="noreferrer">Read the scientific specification</a></p></section>
 <section><h2>9. What would establish predictive validity?</h2><p>A useful validation dataset would connect the same construct’s measured three-dimensional graft/contact geometry, nerve inputs, reinnervation state, raw multichannel recordings, acquisition settings and source-isolation experiments. Transport validation additionally needs vessel or oxygen measurements over time. Calibration and held-out testing should be separated by construct or subject, not merely adjacent waveform samples.</p><div class="rp-table-scroll"><table><thead><tr><th>Future validation target</th><th>Published anchor</th><th>Required comparison &amp; missing data</th></tr></thead><tbody>{BENCHMARK_TARGETS.map(b=><tr><td>{b.label}<br/><span class="rp-source-label">{b.status}</span></td><td>{b.observed} {b.sourceIds.map(id=><a href={'#source-'+id}>[{sourceNumber(id)}] </a>)}</td><td>{b.comparison}<br/>{b.missingData}</td></tr>)}</tbody></table></div><h3>Unresolved endpoints</h3><ul>{MODEL_SCOPE.excluded.map(item=><li>{item}</li>)}</ul><p>Sensory responses to RPNI stimulation have been studied experimentally, including separate perception and discomfort assessments. Those observations do not provide an electrical safety rule or a sensory-threshold model for this recording simulator. <a href="#source-gonzalez-2024">[{sourceNumber('gonzalez-2024')}]</a></p></section>
 <section id="sources"><h2>10. Primary-source evidence dossier</h2><p>Reviewed {EVIDENCE_REVIEWED_AT}. Original human, animal and computational studies remain separately identified. No source below validates the complete browser model, and a listed intervention is not automatically implemented.</p>{SOURCES.map((r,index)=><section class="rp-source" id={'source-'+r.id}><div class="rp-source-index">[{String(index+1).padStart(2,'0')}]<br/>{r.year}</div><div><h3><a href={r.url} target="_blank" rel="noreferrer">{r.title}</a></h3><p class="rp-source-meta">{r.authors} · {r.studyType}<br/>{r.species}<br/>DOI: <a href={'https://doi.org/'+r.doi} target="_blank" rel="noreferrer">{r.doi}</a></p><p><span class="rp-source-label">Reported finding</span>{r.finding}</p><p><span class="rp-source-label">Use in this model</span>{r.modelUse}</p><p><span class="rp-source-label">Boundary</span>{r.limits}</p></div></section>)}</section>
